+## Step-by-Step Integration Guides
+
+### Guide A: Connecting Gemini App, Claude, Cursor & CLI via OAuth 2.1
+
+#### 1. Google Gemini Connected Apps
+1. Open the 👍kanban MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp`) and click **Connect OAuth App**.
+2. Select the **Gemini App** tab to view your MCP OAuth Resource URL (e.g. `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`).
+3. In Google Gemini (gemini.google.com), navigate to **Settings > Connected Apps > Add Custom MCP Server**.
+4. Enter the Resource URL and complete the OAuth 2.1 PKCE authorization flow in the browser popup.
+
+#### 2. Claude Desktop & Claude Code
+1. In the **Connect OAuth App** modal, select the **Claude Code** tab.
+2. For Claude Code CLI, run the auto-generated command:
+   ```bash
+   claude mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent
+   ```
+3. For Claude Desktop, add the server configuration to `claude_desktop_config.json`:
+   ```json
+   {
+     "mcpServers": {
+       "kanban": {
+         "url": "https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent"
+       }
+     }
+   }
+   ```
+
+#### 3. Cursor & VS Code
+1. In Cursor, open **Settings > Features > MCP**.
+2. Add a new MCP server with Server Type `SSE` (or HTTP) and URL `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`.
+3. When prompted, complete the OAuth 2.1 authorization in your default browser.
+
+#### 4. Antigravity & OpenAI Codex CLI
+1. For Google Antigravity CLI, run:
+   ```bash
+   agy mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent --auth oauth
+   ```
+2. For OpenAI Codex CLI, run:
+   ```bash
+   codex mcp add kanban https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent --auth oauth
+   ```
+
+---
+
+### Guide B: Minting Scoped MCP API Keys
+
+1. Navigate to `/settings/mcp` (or `/admin/mcp` for administrative keys).
+2. Click **+ Mint MCP Key** to open the creation modal.
+3. **Configure Key Metadata:**
+   - **Key Name / Label:** Descriptive name (e.g. `CI Bot Runner`).
+   - **Role:** Select `Personal` (for user-bound keys) or `Service Bot` (for headless automation).
+   - **Scope Tier:** Choose `Project`, `Workspace`, or `Global`. For project keys, select the target project.
+   - **Surface:** Select `/mcp/agent` or `/mcp/docs`.
+   - **Expiration:** Choose a preset (30, 60, 90 days, 1 year) or `Unlimited` (requires checking the risk acknowledgment checkbox).
+   - **Capability Mask:** Select a preset (`Full Admin: 255`, `Standard: 31`, `Read-Only: 1`, `Deny-All: 0`) or configure individual bitmask flags.
+   - **CIDR Allowlist:** Optionally specify comma-separated allowed IPv4/IPv6 subnets (e.g. `192.168.1.0/24`).
+   - **Tool Toggles:** Deselect specific tools to disable them for this key.
+4. Click **Mint Key** and copy the secret token immediately. The raw token is write-only and will never be displayed again.
+
+---
+
+### Guide C: Configuring Headless Workload OIDC (GitHub Actions / GitLab CI / Kubernetes)
+
+1. Open `/admin/mcp` and navigate to the **Workload Identity Federation** section.
+2. Click **+ Add Workload Policy** to open the policy creation drawer.
+3. Select a provider preset or enter custom settings:
+   - **GitHub Actions Preset:** Issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`, Subject pattern `repo:my-org/my-repo:ref:refs/heads/main`.
+   - **GitLab CI Preset:** Issuer `https://gitlab.com`, Subject pattern `project_path:my-group/my-project:ref_type:branch:ref:main`.
+   - **Kubernetes Preset:** Issuer `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local`, Subject pattern `system:serviceaccount:default:ci-runner`.
+4. Configure required claims, target surface (`/mcp/agent`), allowed project scopes, capability mask, and token TTL (`max_ttl_seconds <= 86400`).
+5. Save the policy. Headless workloads can now exchange native OIDC tokens for ephemeral MCP tokens via `POST /oauth/token` without static passwords.
+
+---
+
+### Guide D: Governing and Purging OAuth 2.1 Clients
+
+1. Navigate to `/admin/oauth` to access the OAuth Client Governance Studio.
+2. **Inspect Clients:** Use filter pills (`All`, `Active`, `Suspended`, `Revoked`) or search by client name or ID.
+3. **Manage Lifecycle:**
+   - Click **Inspect** to review client registration metadata, redirect URIs, and scopes.
+   - Click **Suspend** to temporarily disable token exchange for a client.
+   - Click **Revoke** to permanently invalidate a client and all associated refresh tokens.
+4. **Single-Transaction Bulk Purge:**
+   - To clean up stale Dynamic Client Registration (DCR) clients created during automated testing or evaluation, click **Purge Stale Clients**.
+   - Review the count of unused clients (clients without active refresh tokens or grants).
+   - Confirm the operation to purge up to thousands of stale clients atomically in a single database transaction.
+
